## Notes / decisions / blockers

**6 (upset factor) — built for every bracket type, not just double elim.** You
scoped it to double elim, but the buckets are projected *placements*, and the
maths does not care how the bracket is shaped — a single-elim bracket produces
the same tiers. Restricting it would have meant threading bracketType into the
component to show less. Say if you want it double-elim only.

**20 (view-only) — one case I could not verify.** The rule is owner-or-admin,
and I confirmed both ends against the live API with my own token: my tournament
returns my id as owner and a visible `admins: []`, somebody else's returns a
different owner and `admins: null`. What I could not test is an *admin who is
not the owner*, because I only have my own account — that path is covered by a
unit test, not by live data. It resolves toward letting them report if start.gg
is unclear, so the failure mode is a spectator typing a score start.gg then
refuses, rather than a real TO locked out at a venue.

**The server test flake is fixed.** It was `Error: socket hang up` (and,
less often, an HTTP parse error), and the cause was supertest rather than the
app: `request(app)` stands up a fresh `app.listen(0)` and closes it around
*every single request*, so a run churns hundreds of short-lived listeners
through the ephemeral port range until one binds a port the kernel has not
finished with. That is why it kept landing on 401-gate tests that do no I/O at
all — the request died in the transport, nowhere near whatever test got blamed.

Measured before fixing, minimal app, 8 concurrent workers: **5 failures in
40,000 requests** listening per-request, **0 in 40,000** listening once. All 7
supertest files now call `testServer(createApp())` once per file and use
`request(server)`. 30 consecutive suite runs clean afterwards.

Two things worth knowing about the fix. Holding one listener for a whole file
means `server.close()` now waits out any socket a test left open, so the
teardown calls `closeAllConnections()` first — without it, a test that opens
the SSE route would fail its *whole file* on a hook timeout that names the
helper rather than the test holding the socket. And because reverting this is
invisible (the suite stays green, it just goes back to being flaky), there is a
guard test that fails if any supertest file stops going through the helper.

**Flaky web tests, noted not fixed.** Two runs of `npm run verify` during this
batch failed a single App test that passes on its own and in its own file
immediately after (`leaves a dead end on screen as NOT REPORTED`, and earlier
`keeps trying when the failure is one that might clear up`). Both drive the
outbox on real timers, so they wait out a real backoff while the rest of the
suite competes for the event loop. I gave two of them a 15s budget already;
if it keeps happening the answer is probably to drive the drain loop with
injected time rather than wall clock. Not chased further today.

**10 (desktop space) — condensed first, then made a modal, and one decision
inside it is yours to overrule.** The report screen was using the plain centred
640px shell while the main screen had a proper full-height one; it now has a
fixed header, its own scrolling body and a pinned action bar. Measured at
1500x950 afterwards there was still a lot of empty margin, so the condition you
set fired and it is a modal over the bracket now.

The decision: both poll effects deliberately stop while a set is open
(`|| selectedSet`), so the bracket behind the modal is genuinely frozen. I left
it frozen and dimmed it, because dimming reads as "paused" while a bright
bracket would be quietly lying. The alternative is to keep polling behind the
modal, which costs start.gg traffic during the one activity that needs to be
fast and moves the bracket under the TO mid-report. Say if you want it live.

